;; Layout conformance corpus: each case is rendered BOTH by cssom.layout and
;; by a real Blink browser (Brave; Chrome is the same engine), and the two
;; are compared on LINE STRUCTURE -- which text ends up on which line, in
;; which order -- rather than on exact pixel coordinates, because this
;; engine has no glyph shaping and its `(long (* 0.6 font-size))` character
;; approximation can never match a real font's metrics. Line structure is
;; the part that is genuinely comparable, and it is exactly the part this
;; engine has historically got wrong.
;;
;; Keep case content SHORT enough not to wrap at the harness width: a
;; wrapped run's break point DOES depend on real font metrics, so those
;; cases are reported separately (:wrapped) instead of being scored as
;; failures for a reason the comparison cannot legitimately judge.
;;
;; A failing case is a MEASUREMENT, not a bug report -- several here are
;; known, documented scope-cuts (floats, tables, <img>/form controls as
;; inline-level). They are in the corpus precisely so the coverage number
;; tells the truth about them.
;;
;; TWO HAZARDS OF THE SHARED PAGE, both found by causing them (round 21):
;;
;; 1. Every case is a `<div class="kotoba-case">` in ONE document, so an
;;    UNBALANCED case swallows the ones after it -- 16 `<div>`s against 15
;;    `</div>`s put the harness's own `<script>` inside that case's root.
;;    Keep tags balanced; there is a corpus-wide balance check.
;; 2. A FLOAT that extends past its own container intrudes on the NEXT
;;    case: measured, one pushed the following case's BFC box 50px right,
;;    one moved the following case's float from x=10 to x=130, and one
;;    turned a one-line paragraph into two. A case that deliberately leaves
;;    a float uncontained must wrap it in an `overflow: hidden` box and say
;;    in a comment that the wrapper is not the subject.
;;
;; Keys: :id :group :html :css, plus
;;   :oracle/blind true -- the oracle structurally CANNOT see this case's
;;     text (generated content: no Range reaches a ::before or a list
;;     marker). Excluded from the LINE score; geometry and computed style
;;     are still compared.
;;   :composite true -- deliberately exercises several mechanisms at once,
;;     so a failure attributes nothing on its own. Read the isolating cases
;;     for the cause.

